Hello, I'm your host, David Barnard, and my guest today is Joseph Choi, a former e commerce marketer who's now helping app developers go viral on TikTok.

7
00:00:51,280 --> 00:01:02,800
On the podcast, I talk with Joseph about what's currently working for apps on TikTok, how to create viral content, and why you should try working with influencers who don't have many followers.

8
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:06,080
Hey, Joseph, thanks so much for joining me on the podcast today.

9
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:07,280
Thanks for having me, David.

10
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:10,240
So we're going to be talking about TikTok.

11
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:16,960
I think a lot of folks are going to be excited to listen to this episode, but before we get into some of the techniques and tricks that are going to be used.

12
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:22,000
Why TikTok and what got you into TikTok marketing?

13
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:23,840
I'll give you a quick background story.

14
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:27,040
When I was in college, I wanted to make money online.

15
00:01:27,360 --> 00:01:29,280
I was bored with my classes, so I went on YouTube.

16
00:01:29,680 --> 00:01:34,320
I went, listened to podcasts, and the business that I found was dropshipping.

17
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:41,680
It's like, it's a fulfillment model for e commerce businesses where you buy the product as soon as the customer buys the product.

18
00:01:41,920 --> 00:01:43,840
So you have razor thin margins.

19
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:44,960
It's not a very good business.

20
00:01:45,600 --> 00:01:49,200
And the only differentiator you have is copywriting and marketing and ads.

21
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:54,000
So I got really good at ads and I noticed that a lot of the e commerce marketers over the years.

22
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:57,760
have been shifting into app development.

23
00:01:58,080 --> 00:02:08,240
And it's really interesting to me to see a lot of the same e commerce tactics that I sort of learned marketing in now being used in the consumer tech apps space.

24
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:17,600
So in the past few months, I've just been meeting a lot more founders that are leveraging TikTok and short form video to go viral with their apps.

25
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:26,000
And I really think it's a great opportunity to get your first, you know, thousand users, ten thousand MRR for subscription apps.

26
00:02:26,320 --> 00:02:27,040
Let's dive in then.

27
00:02:27,360 --> 00:02:30,800
And I wanted to start with why TikTok.

28
00:02:31,200 --> 00:02:32,880
So, you know, there's so many ways.

29
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:39,280
to get attention, press and SEO and starting a blog and like all these different things.

30
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:47,600
But what's unique about TikTok that people are starting to find success and that it is maybe a good place to experiment with zero to one?

31
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:54,560
When you scroll on TikTok and you're scrolling through the videos, the videos you're seeing are not from people that you follow.

32
00:02:55,200 --> 00:03:02,560
I would approximate maybe 95 plus percent of the videos that you scroll through are people that are from creators that you've never seen before.

33
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:08,000
So because of this, there's just a ton of watch time going to brand new accounts, brand new videos.

34
00:03:08,560 --> 00:03:15,760
So as a creator, you can make videos on TikTok from zero followers and get millions of views on your first video.

35
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:18,240
I experienced this once myself.

36
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:19,280
I made a new account.

37
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:24,480
I made a video with zero followers and I got fifty thousand views on the first video.

38
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:26,800
And I was just talking about like San Francisco.

39
00:03:26,960 --> 00:03:30,880
I was living in San Francisco at the time and I just started talking about SF culture.

40
00:03:31,360 --> 00:03:33,200
But people in the app space are doing this too.

41
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:35,520
And I think that's what's unique about TikTok.

42
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:46,320
It's like, you know, before you had to build up a following on your Instagram or your Facebook or if you wanted to do ads, you know, you pay for the content to put it in front of people with TikTok.

43
00:03:46,560 --> 00:03:47,920
You just have to make good quality content.

44
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:49,680
It can go viral from scratch.

45
00:03:49,920 --> 00:03:53,360
Yeah, your, your cost of marketing is, is zero if you do it yourself.

46
00:03:53,520 --> 00:03:58,240
And then if you're paying a creator, you can pay them a flat fee and then get exponential return.

47
00:03:58,640 --> 00:04:07,280
You mentioned that you started in e commerce and it seems like e commerce maybe was a little earlier to the TikTok scene than apps.

48
00:04:07,680 --> 00:04:13,360
What do you think has changed or what do you think has made it more interesting for apps as of late?

49
00:04:13,760 --> 00:04:18,640
So TikTok shop launched in November of 2022.

50
00:04:19,520 --> 00:04:22,400
So it's November 2024 when we're recording this.

51
00:04:22,640 --> 00:04:25,040
It's only been two years since TikTok shop launched.

52
00:04:25,040 --> 00:04:30,080
TikTok shop is basically an affili affiliate platform for e commerce products on TikTok.

53
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:34,080
So, as a brand owner, you can upload your e commerce product to TikTok Shop.

54
00:04:34,480 --> 00:04:36,480
You set an affiliate commission percentage.

55
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:49,040
So, some people do 15 to 20 percent, and then creators will automatically can just sign up for your to affiliate for your product and start making videos and the payments and everything is handled through TikTok.

56
00:04:49,680 --> 00:04:59,680
So, in the beginning, this was a huge gold rush for brands because you could just upload your product and then pay commission only to these TikTok creators.

57
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:10,160
So, over the past two years, there's been a big rush on the creator side for all these creators that are now, they see how much money you can make on TikTok shop.

58
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:15,440
You know, these 15% commissions add up very quickly if you go viral with a TikTok.

59
00:05:15,920 --> 00:05:25,440
So now there's this whole new class of creators that is really good at making video content that's that not only gets views but is high converting.

60
00:05:25,680 --> 00:05:28,640
And actually, you know, it's not just views as a vanity metric.

61
00:05:28,800 --> 00:05:34,640
Like the, it's actually driving millions and millions of dollars of GMV just through these TikTok shop creators.

62
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:46,240
So I think the space as a whole of influencer and creator marketing has evolved and people have invented new tactics for storytelling and hooks and creative CTAs.

63
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:51,600
So TikTok Shop has definitely fueled a lot of the innovation in the marketing side.

64
00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:56,880
One of the companies that I think is really interesting in this space, uh, in the e commerce space is Tabs Chocolate.

65
00:05:57,040 --> 00:06:02,240
So they've they went from zero to ten million in revenue in a year just on TikTok Shop.

66
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:11,120
And the founder of that, Oliver, he put the Tabs Chocolate product on hold and he switched to apps.

67
00:06:11,840 --> 00:06:16,400
So then he launched an app called Study Buddy and it helps students with their homework.

68
00:06:16,640 --> 00:06:19,360
You know, it's an AI app, but he used, he was very transparent.

69
00:06:19,520 --> 00:06:30,800
He built it in public and he was was saying, hey, I'm just going to use all my knowledge of e commerce and just do the same marketing but for this app because it's higher margin, it's higher exit multiples, higher retention.

70
00:06:31,440 --> 00:06:36,240
So, you know, once he started doing that, then a bunch of apps started catching on and doing the same thing.

71
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:38,960
And I think it's, yeah, I think this is the new wave.

72
00:06:39,200 --> 00:06:45,840
I think like naturally, apps have always grown through different ways, but I think TikTok is, I think TikTok is the new thing.

73
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:52,880
And it's interesting, I mean, it's not that apps are, are suddenly going viral on TikTok or that nobody has tried to make it work before.

74
00:06:53,200 --> 00:06:57,920
Because, like, famously Widgetsmith went viral on TikTok in 2020.

75
00:06:58,720 --> 00:07:02,960
So four years ago, it went viral, went, you know, shot to number one in the app store.

76
00:07:03,520 --> 00:07:06,160
We've actually had David Smith, the developer of that on the podcast.

77
00:07:06,400 --> 00:07:09,600
And we've talked to other apps that are doing TikTok marketing.

78
00:07:10,480 --> 00:07:21,040
But it seems like maybe the why now kind of thing, why is it starting to blow up is that these e commerce folks have really kind of pioneered the techniques that are now actually going to start working better for apps.

79
00:07:21,280 --> 00:07:21,920
I think that's right.

80
00:07:22,160 --> 00:07:22,240
Yeah.

81
00:07:22,720 --> 00:07:25,840
I wanted to dive into your understanding of the algorithm.

82
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:31,360
And you kind of already mentioned how the for you page drives so much of this.

83
00:07:31,920 --> 00:07:34,400
But I wanted to dive a little deeper into that contrast.

84
00:07:34,880 --> 00:07:36,560
SEO, you know, is so hard.

85
00:07:37,280 --> 00:07:42,160
to get your page ranked because almost any topic's already so inundated.

86
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:57,120
As you mentioned, Instagram and Twitter and other typical social media platforms, Facebook, you have to build up an audience before you can actually see scale is just such a different experience.

87
00:07:57,360 --> 00:08:05,680
And so is there anything else about the algorithm that you think makes it so effective for going viral and as a marketing channel?

88
00:08:05,920 --> 00:08:14,960
There's a distinction between influencers and what I like to call, I guess, like scrappy UGC creators in the traditional sense of what an influencer is.

89
00:08:15,440 --> 00:08:42,240
like, I think influencers became a thing around the Instagram era, maybe a little bit before, but basically they have like an influencer is really, if you think about it in a, like an analogy for creating a movie, a movie production, which is what TikTok videos are basically just short movies, you have like you have the director, you have the actor, and then you have the audience, you know, the distribution and an influencer plays all three of those roles.

90
00:08:42,400 --> 00:08:46,000
They're the talent that shows up in the video, they're the creative director.

91
00:08:46,320 --> 00:08:50,320
They actually think of the video ideas and then edit it and everything.

92
00:08:50,800 --> 00:08:56,560
And then they also have an audience that pre existsing audience that they've built trust with over the years through their content.

93
00:08:57,200 --> 00:09:07,760
But what TikTok allows you to do as a brand owner or an app developer is you can hire a creator to play just the role of the actor.

94
00:09:08,160 --> 00:09:15,360
So if you know how to do marketing, you can give them a script that you know will perform well on the for you page.

95
00:09:15,600 --> 00:09:17,600
So now you're playing the role of the director.

96
00:09:18,160 --> 00:09:23,680
And then now TikTok itself is playing the role of the audience and the distribution.

97
00:09:23,840 --> 00:09:26,160
You don't need a pre existing audience.

98
00:09:26,560 --> 00:09:31,280
And the creators are willing to take this deal because a lot of them are undermonetetized.

99
00:09:31,440 --> 00:09:32,560
They're just making videos.

100
00:09:33,120 --> 00:09:41,280
Many of them get reached out to by e commerce products, these big CPG companies that say, Hey, I'll give you a product for free and then please make a video for us.

101
00:09:41,840 --> 00:09:47,760
And they're like, That doesn't seem like a great deal, but fine, I'll do it because what else am I going to do?

102
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:51,520
I'm going to make the videos anyway, may as well make a little bit of money out of it.

103
00:09:52,080 --> 00:10:01,200
So if you're willing to put a little bit more money into creator marketing, I think the for you page really enables you to be scrappier with it.

104
00:10:01,440 --> 00:10:08,320
That's a great analogy that instead of having to build an audience, it's the content itself that generates the audience through.

105
00:10:08,480 --> 00:10:09,760
through TikTok's algorithm.

106
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:16,160
Are there any specific things to the algorithm that you're striving for to help it go viral?

107
00:10:16,480 --> 00:10:38,080
Is it just about being engaging or is it like getting more likes or other inputs that you've seen to the algorithm that set aside the content itself, because like we'll dive into what makes great content, but is there anything specific to the algorithm that helps it go viral other than the content itself?

108
00:10:38,720 --> 00:10:40,240
Short answer is no.

109
00:10:41,040 --> 00:10:42,400
It is just the content itself.

110
00:10:42,880 --> 00:10:50,560
What I will say is I'm very skeptical of anyone who claims to know what exactly the TikTok algorithm is, it's always changing.

111
00:10:50,800 --> 00:10:51,760
You know, it's shifting.

112
00:10:51,840 --> 00:10:54,240
Like sometimes people will say shares matter more.

113
00:10:54,800 --> 00:10:59,760
Sometimes people will say likes matter more watch time retention comments.

114
00:11:00,720 --> 00:11:08,320
But if you just think about it from a higher level, like think about if you're TikTok, if you own TikTok, how would you want to design the algorithm?

115
00:11:08,480 --> 00:11:12,320
So like TikTok makes its money from advertising and from TikTok shop.

116
00:11:12,640 --> 00:11:20,240
So if you want to make the most money as TikTok, you want people to watch more videos for longer, just like any other social media platform.

117
00:11:20,720 --> 00:11:30,640
So if you want people to watch more videos for longer, then you want to incentivize people people to make content that people watch the video to its completion and then go on to the next video and then watch that to completion.

118
00:11:31,200 --> 00:11:36,640
You want people to like and enjoy watching the video and have some sort of emotional reaction to watching the video.

119
00:11:36,960 --> 00:11:44,240
So when people say, Oh, it's just about making good content, what that really means is the hook has to be good, the retention has to be good.

120
00:11:44,320 --> 00:11:48,720
And like at the bottom line is, you know, someone has to feel something when they watch the video.

121
00:11:49,040 --> 00:12:01,280
And I imagine the algorithm is probably fairly sophisticated as well in that sometimes it's going to feed you a 15 second video and sometimes it's going to feed you a 2 minute video.

122
00:12:03,840 --> 00:12:05,680
video will hit if it's engaging.

123
00:12:06,160 --> 00:12:13,280
If we think about it through the basic human experience of TikTok, it's like driving that, those little mini dopamine rushes.

124
00:12:13,760 --> 00:12:18,160
And so the algorithm is maybe taking so many different things into account.

125
00:12:18,320 --> 00:12:24,560
As you said, that no one thing is always going to work because it's going to depend on the, on the context.

126
00:12:24,800 --> 00:12:37,680
Like is the person, is it 2 am and they're just like dopamine hit and they need 15 seconds videos or is it or are they more in a state where they can engage with a two minute video?

127
00:12:38,160 --> 00:12:48,400
And so it's probably like so context dependent what TikTok is going to feed people that trying to guess at it is, like you said, it's to make people feel something and that's going to be more successful.

128
00:12:49,280 --> 00:12:53,040
Does having followers on TikTok give you any kind of a leg up?

129
00:12:53,360 --> 00:13:05,760
Like if you've got a million followers on TikTok, are you guaranteed to at least get more of a shot or do you feel like even with zero followers, videos can go viral and it doesn't matter as much as you might guess?

130
00:13:06,080 --> 00:13:06,160
Yeah.

131
00:13:06,400 --> 00:13:18,800
If you have a million followers on TikTok, that does give you a leg up for sure because, you know, I I think I gave an estimate, maybe 95% of the videos you watch on the for you page are brand new accounts that you've never seen before, but there's also a following tab.

132
00:13:19,360 --> 00:13:23,680
So if you scroll to the left, you can watch videos that from people that you follow.

133
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:29,360
So you do reach an audience when you post a video from an account that has followers.

134
00:13:29,760 --> 00:13:30,880
You do get a boost for sure.

135
00:13:31,200 --> 00:13:34,640
It's just that the vast majority of the watch time comes from the for you page.

136
00:13:34,800 --> 00:13:38,640
So it's easier to go viral than it was before.

137
00:13:39,120 --> 00:13:40,000
So you have a leg up.

138
00:13:40,320 --> 00:13:46,320
And are there specific strategies that you've seen for apps be successful in growing an audience?

139
00:13:46,880 --> 00:14:08,160
Or do you think that the focus is more just content and should an account like have a point of view and model some of the old style influencer that is this account is all about?, you know, like if I created one for my weather app, like, should I strive to create something people would actually want to follow by sharing interesting content in addition to any like pitch kind of stuff?

140
00:14:08,560 --> 00:14:15,440
Or do you think it's more important to just really focus on, on each video being as potentially viral as possible?

141
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:16,080
Yeah.

142
00:14:16,480 --> 00:14:18,560
This seems kind of like a business strategy question.

143
00:14:18,800 --> 00:14:28,640
Like, I feel like the long term goal is always to build a great brand because that contributes to better SEO and ASO and just general word of mouth and brand love.

144
00:14:28,960 --> 00:14:30,880
So I think it's always great to build a brand.

145
00:14:31,280 --> 00:14:36,800
I've seen that Instagram reels is better for that because there's actually a homep feed where it's just people that you follow.

146
00:14:37,520 --> 00:14:41,200
So it's a lot easier to reach your own followers from an Instagram account.

147
00:14:41,760 --> 00:14:48,400
So yeah, I think, you know, having a legitimate brand beyond just like going viral every day, it's always good.

148
00:14:48,640 --> 00:14:50,240
Yeah, I think that's always a good thing to have.

149
00:14:50,480 --> 00:14:50,480
Yeah.

150
00:14:51,200 --> 00:14:55,040
What kind of apps do you think this works for?

151
00:14:55,360 --> 00:14:58,160
You and I actually talked about this directly in relation to my weather app.

152
00:14:58,400 --> 00:15:10,560
I forget the exact quote, but it was something like, yeah, I just don't know, like, how viral you're going to be able to go with a weather app and like, you know, productivity apps, maybe Widgetsmith being an exception.

153
00:15:11,200 --> 00:15:13,680
Is it harder for productivity in those types of apps.

154
00:15:13,760 --> 00:15:17,120
And then which apps do you think are the absolute best fit for this type of marketing?

155
00:15:17,600 --> 00:15:17,680
Yeah.

156
00:15:17,920 --> 00:15:19,120
So TikTok is a video platform.

157
00:15:19,520 --> 00:15:25,200
So apps that have some sort of visual element or highly visual wow factor do very well on TikTok.

158
00:15:25,440 --> 00:15:40,240
So things like consumer social dating, personal finance, health and wellness, if you have any of those apps that have some sort of strong visual component, it's easier to make viral content about those apps, but you can be creative with it.

159
00:15:40,480 --> 00:15:49,040
If you look at the TikTok ad library and you go to the top performing ads of all time, they're all utility apps.

160
00:15:49,360 --> 00:15:50,640
They're just, you know, things like.

161
00:15:51,360 --> 00:15:57,120
phone storage cleaners and AI spam calling blockers and things like that.

162
00:15:57,440 --> 00:16:01,120
One niche is that I've seen do really well is just productivity apps.

163
00:16:01,200 --> 00:16:11,680
So there's this app because if you think about the types of content that exist on TikTok, there's a lot of people that talk about things from their personal experience, like ADHD or productivity motivation.

164
00:16:12,080 --> 00:16:14,400
There's a lot of content that exists in that niche already.

165
00:16:14,800 --> 00:16:24,720
So partnering with creators in that niche or just copying the content that already works in that niche, it can apply very well to things like productivity apps and utility apps.

166
00:16:24,800 --> 00:16:29,200
Like you're saying, if you think about in general, like what type of marketing works as a whole.

167
00:16:29,360 --> 00:16:31,280
It's like health, wealth, relationships.

168
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,080
If it has to do with either of those things.

169
00:16:34,720 --> 00:16:44,320
So, for example, productivity is somewhat about wealth, somewhat about health, and you can attach that type of storytelling in your marketing to the app.

170
00:16:44,800 --> 00:16:46,960
Weather is that health, wealth, relationships.

171
00:16:47,760 --> 00:16:49,120
I think weather is a difficult one.

172
00:16:49,760 --> 00:17:01,280
You were saying, you know, looking at the top ads, these like phone cleaner and stuff like that, but are those going viral or is that like those are successful as ads because a lot of money is put behind them?

173
00:17:01,360 --> 00:17:03,360
Because there's those two kinds of things.

174
00:17:03,680 --> 00:17:12,880
Like a lot of what you've been talking about on Twitter and you know, you have a this community you're building up of apps working to go viral and use TikTok as a marketing channel.

175
00:17:13,280 --> 00:17:15,440
But there's the free marketing go viral.

176
00:17:15,680 --> 00:17:20,720
And then there's like put money behind a piece of content and that performing well.

177
00:17:21,360 --> 00:17:34,800
So do you see those kind of productivity tools and cleaner storage and stuff, are those actually going viral or is it that those just make so much money, the creators of those apps are able to put so much money behind them on spend?

178
00:17:35,280 --> 00:17:39,680
They're primarily getting their users through paid acquisition because they can't go viral on TikTok.

179
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:40,720
That was my point there.

180
00:17:41,200 --> 00:17:42,960
I think the maybe I should make a distinction.

181
00:17:43,040 --> 00:17:44,400
There's productivity apps.

182
00:17:45,280 --> 00:17:50,560
that solve a deep pain point about your daily motivation and building habits.

183
00:17:50,960 --> 00:17:57,120
It's a very emotional, emotionally tied product versus a productivity app that cleans your phone storage.

184
00:17:57,600 --> 00:17:59,360
It's a somewhat emotional problem, but it's not.

185
00:17:59,520 --> 00:18:00,320
It's not as deep.

186
00:18:00,480 --> 00:18:06,240
It's more of a you see an ad and the ad has a good creative and you're just like, Oh, that seems useful.

187
00:18:06,400 --> 00:18:07,120
Let me download that.

188
00:18:07,360 --> 00:18:15,040
And then there's a paywall and then you pay the paywall versus a TikTok that says, Hey, are you struggling with motivation?

