00:35:54,160 --> 00:35:58,960
So they say, you know, the first slide is, so it's all wedding based, weirdly enough.

379
00:35:59,120 --> 00:36:04,720
So they say, here's five controversial rules that we had at our wedding recently.

380
00:36:04,880 --> 00:36:06,720
And then there's this image of, you know, a wedding.

381
00:36:07,440 --> 00:36:08,480
And so that's a great hook.

382
00:36:08,640 --> 00:36:10,560
It's a very negative, interesting hook.

383
00:36:10,800 --> 00:36:12,720
People are like, oh, what are the controversial rules?

384
00:36:13,360 --> 00:36:15,200
And then the rules are controversial.

385
00:36:15,360 --> 00:36:19,040
It's like, oh, no kids at the wedding or no taking pictures.

386
00:36:19,840 --> 00:36:29,360
And then the no taking pictures kind of hints at the last slide, which is, oh, by the way, I had the guests use the POV app from the App Store.

387
00:36:29,840 --> 00:36:38,320
So instead of taking pictures normally with cameras, they have to use this disposable camera app and it created a better, you know, vibe for taking pictures at the wedding.

388
00:36:38,640 --> 00:36:44,160
So they have multiple accounts making these slideshow videos and the app, the CTA there is.

389
00:36:44,160 --> 00:36:47,040
is, you know, I had the guests use the POV app.

390
00:36:47,360 --> 00:36:54,000
So it's a they directly mention the app, but it's part of a storyline rather than saying, hey, go download this thing.

391
00:36:54,240 --> 00:36:56,000
Yeah, and it sounds more organic too, right?

392
00:36:56,240 --> 00:37:05,520
Because it's like, here's our five crazy rules at our wedding, one, two, three, four, and I used the app to enforce rule number four or whatever.

393
00:37:05,760 --> 00:37:08,560
So yeah, that's, man, that's super slick.

394
00:37:08,800 --> 00:37:11,520
One more is, this one's really good for talking head content.

395
00:37:11,760 --> 00:37:30,560
So if you have creators, if you get a hundred creators to just start storytelling on camera, a good hook that a lot of people are using in the e commerce space and the app space now is, am I the only one who that saw that viral video from that XYZ authority figure about this thing?

396
00:37:31,600 --> 00:37:45,600
And it's just like the viral video might exist, it might not, but it's like it's also ambiguous enough to where, yeah, of course there's gonna be a lot of viral videos about every topic, but just saying like, oh, am I the only one that saw the viral video?

397
00:37:45,680 --> 00:37:46,880
It's just it's just a good hook.

398
00:37:47,040 --> 00:37:48,480
And eventually it will get saturated.

399
00:37:48,640 --> 00:37:54,320
Like everyone will say the same hook and then people will start to catch on that, oh, this is not organic.

400
00:37:54,720 --> 00:37:57,920
But it is a trend that I think is still quite active right now.

401
00:37:58,240 --> 00:37:58,320
Yeah.

402
00:37:58,480 --> 00:38:07,280
Another, another CTA is I've seen this one in the ADHD niche going back to that, but you just start with a pain point and then give useful tips.

403
00:38:07,440 --> 00:38:15,360
So you say, oh, if you have trouble with ADHD, here's this hack I found, or here's some tips on how to deal with ADHD.

404
00:38:15,600 --> 00:38:19,360
And then you give actual tips and then you say, oh, also I use this app.

405
00:38:19,760 --> 00:38:20,800
It doesn't have to be at the end.

406
00:38:20,880 --> 00:38:30,880
You could use the app as tip number three out of five, or, you know, but the basis of the video is that here's a bunch of here's these hacks that I, or life hack, or ADHD hack.

407
00:38:31,040 --> 00:38:43,760
Actually, if you go on TikTok and you search hashtag ADHD hack, you'll get lots of examples of these videos of people sharing their ADHD hacks, and you can replicate some of those copywriting structures.

408
00:38:44,160 --> 00:38:55,120
What's interesting to me too is, as we've been talking through all this, and you said it earlier, is that some apps specifically build features to go viral.

409
00:38:55,840 --> 00:39:17,840
And I wonder if that's actually a good product strategy is that, you know, if you're building features that people care about that are interesting that have some natural pain point that's going to bring people back into the app, like if you're building features that don't have any kind of hook that's going to go viral on TikTok?

410
00:39:18,080 --> 00:39:23,200
Like, are you really like building something valuable for folks that they're actually going to use?

411
00:39:23,360 --> 00:39:45,280
I mean, that's what so many apps I talk to really struggle with retention because yes, you created some great solution for ADHD, but it's a huge pain to use or it doesn't have that like emotional connection that's going to bring you back to it or it doesn't have that wow factor that's going to inspire people to use it.

412
00:39:45,760 --> 00:40:00,800
If this whole strategy and finding things that are likely to go viral on TikTok is actually a great way to do feature discovery., product discovery, and think of new apps to build, think of new features to build through these frameworks.

413
00:40:01,120 --> 00:40:06,960
You've essentially shared over this whole podcast about like what people are going to connect with 100%.

414
00:40:07,120 --> 00:40:24,400
I've even seen people build apps in the completely opposite direction where they do the marketing first just for a waitlist and they talk about the app and the concept of it and show Figma screenshots and the app doesn't even exist yet and they just get people to sign up for a waitlist and that's instant product validation.

415
00:40:24,640 --> 00:40:34,640
If you get a bunch of views on a TikTok that talks about your app, you get a bunch of sign-ups for for your waitlist, then you can build the app after you market it., there's this app called Breezy.

416
00:40:35,040 --> 00:40:36,320
The founders in the community.

417
00:40:36,560 --> 00:40:44,080
It's basically a social note sharing app, but it really resonates with a certain demographic of young Gen Z people.

418
00:40:44,640 --> 00:41:00,240
Yeah, she just makes founder led content talking about the app itself, showing Figma screenshots, some beta screenshots, and then it's a waitlist and she made a video that was 15 seconds long just talking about the app and got, I think, 5,000 waitlist signups.

419
00:41:00,720 --> 00:41:03,040
So yeah, that was that was Breezy, the Breezy app.

420
00:41:03,200 --> 00:41:10,960
I wonder if that's actually even a good strategy too, because as far as hooks go, that's another kind of hook, like being an engineer.

421
00:41:10,960 --> 00:41:15,440
Being an insider, being in the know, being early to something that's going to blow up.

422
00:41:15,920 --> 00:41:19,120
Yeah, I wonder if that's actually a fun hook for folks to play with.

423
00:41:19,440 --> 00:41:22,960
It's like as you're building a new feature, tease it in that way.

424
00:41:23,120 --> 00:41:32,240
And like, hey, you want to get on our beta or you want to join the wait list for when this comes out versus building it first and then creating the marketing.

425
00:41:33,040 --> 00:41:34,320
That could be a really cool strategy.

426
00:41:34,640 --> 00:41:35,920
And then you kind of like do that.

427
00:41:36,080 --> 00:41:42,880
And like, like you're saying, it's like that's how you validate whether or not these features are going to hit or not.

428
00:41:43,360 --> 00:41:44,240
I've built so many.

429
00:41:44,880 --> 00:41:53,040
I've launched twenty two, three apps over the last sixteen years and some were like hits and some were total duds.

430
00:41:53,600 --> 00:42:02,160
There wasn't a ton of correlation between, you know, the amount of time spent or like I probably should have found better ways to do product validation in advance.

431
00:42:02,640 --> 00:42:11,520
And so, so for, you know, side project apps today for big apps that are looking for new features, that's a really cool idea for doing some product validation ahead.

432
00:42:12,080 --> 00:42:13,600
See if it goes viral on TikTok.

433
00:42:13,680 --> 00:42:15,920
And if it doesn't, maybe it's not the feature to build next.

434
00:42:16,400 --> 00:42:18,400
I just want to give one more thing relating to this.

435
00:42:18,880 --> 00:42:23,680
This is an infinitely repeatable tactic that just it happens year over year.

436
00:42:24,000 --> 00:42:25,360
It's just Spotify wrapped.

437
00:42:25,520 --> 00:42:26,720
If you just remake Spotify.

438
00:42:27,120 --> 00:42:29,280
raps, but for XYZ niche.

439
00:42:29,600 --> 00:42:32,560
That concept goes viral every single year reliably.

440
00:42:32,720 --> 00:42:35,200
Like the most recent one is the Verse app.

441
00:42:35,360 --> 00:42:51,120
It's a consumer social app and they just did a design, they generated an AI image bedroom based on the style of the music that you listen to and then they used that as a hook for the consumer social app and it went super, super viral.

442
00:42:51,200 --> 00:42:53,280
I think they hit number one in the app store for a few days.

443
00:42:53,680 --> 00:42:53,760
Wow.

444
00:42:54,400 --> 00:42:58,000
So the last thing I wanted to talk about was working with influencers.

445
00:42:58,560 --> 00:43:04,400
So I'm a good example that I don't think me getting on TikTok is going to sell.

446
00:43:06,560 --> 00:43:15,840
Yeah, middle aged man, you know, talking about apps, like there'll be a lot of folks like you who will in your framing be the actor, the director and kind of do everything.

447
00:43:16,160 --> 00:43:19,520
And I think for people who can pull that off, that's gonna be a ton of fun.

448
00:43:19,920 --> 00:43:23,440
But for a lot of folks, like, they're not gonna be able to do this themselves.

449
00:43:23,920 --> 00:43:30,240
And so finding creators to work with is gonna be a huge part of making this successful.

450
00:43:30,480 --> 00:43:36,720
So yeah, what are your top tips for finding and working with creators to help implement what we've been talking about?

451
00:43:37,200 --> 00:43:40,960
I think influencer marketing, traditional influencer marketing is very expensive.

452
00:43:41,040 --> 00:43:46,320
If you reach out to some of these creators on who have, you know, more than a hundred thousand followers.

453
00:43:46,560 --> 00:43:50,080
Many of them will hit you back with their media kit and it's all.

454
00:43:50,640 --> 00:43:57,440
Sometimes they even have an agent, a talent agent that inflates the prices even more, charges you thousands of dollars for a single video.

455
00:43:57,760 --> 00:43:58,800
You want to avoid those people.

456
00:43:59,440 --> 00:44:02,640
They can be good to work with if it's a really, really strong audience fit.

457
00:44:02,960 --> 00:44:10,320
But if you want to get a lot more ROI in a scrappy way, I like going after very low follower count creators.

458
00:44:10,560 --> 00:44:24,240
So finding people that have less than fifty thousand followers who maybe hit one or two viral videos in their time on TikTok, but maybe don't go consistently viral because if they're going consistently viral they're going to be more expensive.

459
00:44:24,880 --> 00:44:30,080
But if they hit one or two viral videos, they know the feeling of what it takes to go viral.

460
00:44:30,560 --> 00:44:34,720
Like they've probably thought about, oh, why did that video go so viral?

461
00:44:34,880 --> 00:44:39,280
Oh, like it's the way that I said these words in these ways, the way that I did the hook.

462
00:44:39,920 --> 00:44:41,440
So they sort of know what it takes.

463
00:44:41,920 --> 00:44:46,960
And then lastly, it's just like human touch, just like look at how they present themselves.

464
00:44:47,120 --> 00:44:48,400
Are they charismatic on camera?

465
00:44:48,800 --> 00:44:56,800
If they're charismatic on camera, they have low followers count, they know how to make viral videos but are not super monetized with sponsored videos yet.

466
00:44:57,280 --> 00:44:58,880
That's the perfect person you want to reach.

467
00:44:59,360 --> 00:44:59,520
out to.

468
00:44:59,920 --> 00:45:02,560
They're probably not making that much money from brand deals.

469
00:45:03,520 --> 00:45:08,560
So you can pay them cash and they'll gladly, they'll probably gladly take it.

470
00:45:08,720 --> 00:45:22,960
It'll take a little bit more training, you know, teaching them about the app and telling them how to structure the video script and things like that, but yeah, I think finding low follower account but charismatic creators is the way to go.

471
00:45:23,280 --> 00:45:31,680
And then how would you suggest structuring payment expectations in those kind of low follower accounts?

472
00:45:31,920 --> 00:45:35,360
They're probably aspiring creators, not, you know, they don't have an agent.

473
00:45:35,600 --> 00:45:42,800
Do you write a contract like what do you offer the creators and how do you structure the deal to make it work?

474
00:45:43,120 --> 00:45:43,200
Yeah.

475
00:45:43,440 --> 00:45:49,680
So many of these creators are used to getting reached out by these big CPG, you know, e commerce companies.

476
00:45:49,920 --> 00:45:51,520
They just do gift, gift-gifted campaigns.

477
00:45:51,680 --> 00:45:54,160
So they just get a product, a free product in exchange for a video.

478
00:45:54,480 --> 00:46:10,320
So in your outreach message, whether it's an email or a DM, if you just say, I want to pay you, like explicitly say, I want to pay you for a video in cash, you don't have to say how much, you know, you can figure out negotiating the rates later, but say explicitly, I want to pay you for a video.

479
00:46:10,800 --> 00:46:12,400
My app is, you know, does this.

480
00:46:12,560 --> 00:46:14,000
I think your audience would be a great fit.

481
00:46:14,160 --> 00:46:14,800
I really like your content.

482
00:46:15,200 --> 00:46:21,760
And then typically how you structure that deal is, you know, as I was saying before, the CTA in these videos is not always link in bio.

483
00:46:22,320 --> 00:46:26,080
So there's not going to be attribution for a lot of these videos.

484
00:46:26,640 --> 00:46:31,680
So what you actually do is you give them bonuses for views.

485
00:46:32,080 --> 00:46:35,520
So if they get a bunch of views, then you can pay them per view.

486
00:46:36,080 --> 00:46:39,920
And then in addition, you can give them a base rate for making videos.

487
00:46:40,400 --> 00:46:48,720
Another thing is, since they're such a low follower count creator and you want them to go viral on the for you page, you can have them make a brand new account.

488
00:46:48,880 --> 00:46:51,200
So you can have them make a new TikTok account.

489
00:46:52,160 --> 00:46:58,320
And name it something else that's, you know, a little bit more branded towards your app, have them post on it every day.

490
00:46:58,640 --> 00:47:03,840
So a lot of these deal structures are you can have them post one TikTok per day for a month.

491
00:47:04,000 --> 00:47:12,800
So thirty videos a month and the cost on that is anywhere from I've seen $500 all the way up to $3,000.

492
00:47:13,360 --> 00:47:18,080
So at $3,000 that would be $100 per video, which is still like fairly cheap.

493
00:47:18,320 --> 00:47:23,840
How much to pay just basically just depends on the level of experience and how much ROI you expect to get out of it.

494
00:47:24,000 --> 00:47:34,480
I have a friend, member of the community, who's paid a one creator $3,000 a month and they got their first 20 K MRR from just one creator just posting every day.

495
00:47:34,880 --> 00:47:36,800
So the retainer, you get what you pay for.

496
00:47:36,960 --> 00:47:42,640
You know, if you pay for a really good creator, it will pay dividends, but then the incentivization is also good too.

497
00:47:42,880 --> 00:47:57,200
So a typical rate that I've seen for that is $1,000 per million views, or you can cut it where it's $500 per 500,000 views, which works out to be $1 CPM, which is very, very cheap.

498
00:47:57,440 --> 00:48:00,960
And you can play around with the rates depending on what the creator wants.

499
00:48:01,360 --> 00:48:05,520
The thing about paying a CPM though is it has to actually deliver.

500
00:48:05,840 --> 00:48:17,120
And so if they create something that goes insanely viral but doesn't actually send people to the app or it sends people to the app that aren't going to be payers.

501
00:48:17,840 --> 00:48:20,240
I could see easily getting pretty upside down.

502
00:48:20,480 --> 00:48:23,200
Have you seen any examples of that or have you experienced that yourself?

503
00:48:23,440 --> 00:48:29,040
Like you do this on a CPM basis and that it goes crazy viral, gets five million views.

504
00:48:29,200 --> 00:48:36,560
Now you're on the hook for five grand, but then the CTA wasn't very strong and like you don't make much money on it.

505
00:48:36,880 --> 00:48:37,520
Yeah, totally.

506
00:48:37,760 --> 00:48:41,360
I mean that's why like the creator brief is like very, very important.

507
00:48:41,680 --> 00:48:46,640
You know, you should agree on all the things that are required from every single video.

508
00:48:47,280 --> 00:48:58,320
So if you know, you know, generally what your strategy is and what you think will be high converting videos, then you should give those guidelines and say you have to have the CTA in this way.

509
00:48:59,040 --> 00:49:03,600
So definitely important to make sure that the videos are viral and high converting.

510
00:49:03,840 --> 00:49:03,920
Yeah.

511
00:49:04,480 --> 00:49:12,400
Earlier, you mentioned that in e commerce, part of what really blew up was the affiliate model with TikTok shops.

512
00:49:13,200 --> 00:49:18,400
Do you think there's potential for that on TikTok with apps doing affiliates?

513
00:49:18,480 --> 00:49:26,960
Like I've actually talked to and probably have on the podcast at some point, the founder of Go Market Me and what they're trying to do do is build a platform for affiliates.

514
00:49:27,120 --> 00:49:29,120
Like, in what cases do you think it would work?

515
00:49:29,360 --> 00:49:36,160
What do you think are the potential there of actually just doing a RevShare where essentially infinite upside?

516
00:49:36,400 --> 00:49:50,560
Like if someone created that video and instead of getting paid five thousand dollars to get five million views, if that five million views actually generated one hundred thousand dollars and you're giving a fifty percent of, you know, first conversion or whatever and they can make fifty K.

517
00:49:50,880 --> 00:49:57,680
I mean, it seems like for a lot of creators this opportunity to make infinite money would be more interesting, but it's tough.

518
00:49:57,680 --> 00:50:00,320
So yeah, what are your thoughts on affiliate on TikTok?

519
00:50:00,560 --> 00:50:01,600
I think it's great.

520
00:50:02,800 --> 00:50:06,160
the gold standard of marketing for as the brand owner.

521
00:50:06,320 --> 00:50:17,040
Like obviously, it would be great to have zero risk upfront and just have people do free marketing for you and only pay if they generate revenue for you.

522
00:50:17,680 --> 00:50:22,320
The problem is, yeah, like TikTok is consumers are very savvy.

523
00:50:22,560 --> 00:50:24,400
They don't really like clicking links and bio.

524
00:50:25,120 --> 00:50:28,080
They want to feel like, you know, this video is just telling them about an app.

525
00:50:28,400 --> 00:50:30,240
They go search on the app store themselves.

526
00:50:30,400 --> 00:50:31,120
They download it.

527
00:50:31,600 --> 00:50:33,360
I think it can work for influencer marketing.

528
00:50:34,000 --> 00:50:41,440
If you have an influencer who has an established audience and they're getting a cut of the link and bio clicks.

529
00:50:42,240 --> 00:50:47,920
I think that has always been and will always be a good way to partner with creators.

530
00:50:48,160 --> 00:50:51,920
If it's like traditional influencer marketing, they have an audience, they have a lot of trust.

531
00:50:52,320 --> 00:50:55,760
But typically with those deals, there's always a base rate in addition.

532
00:50:56,160 --> 00:51:01,920
I think there's a talent constraint or there's a supply constraint of good creator talent.

533
00:51:02,240 --> 00:51:07,440
I think creators actually have, like the big creators, like with audiences, they have a lot more power than the brands.

534
00:51:07,520 --> 00:51:13,520
They're being reached out to by tons of brands all the time competing for their attention and doing deals.

535
00:51:13,840 --> 00:51:18,800
So that's why I sort of advocate for the scrapy creator marketing, non-traditional.

536
00:51:19,280 --> 00:51:25,120
influencer marketing because in that equation, I think the supply demand is reversed.

537
00:51:25,200 --> 00:51:30,960
I think there's a lot more creators that are underpaid that brands can start paying.

538
00:51:31,360 --> 00:51:38,720
So TikTok shop for apps can definitely exist within like a certain context of influencer marketing.

539
00:51:39,120 --> 00:51:41,920
I've actually been playing with offer codes recently.

540
00:51:42,560 --> 00:51:49,280
It's something, again, I'll probably do a whole podcast on or, or, uh, write something up about the experiments I've been doing.

541
00:51:49,600 --> 00:51:54,800
But what's interesting about offer codes is that you get 100% attribution on those.

542
00:51:55,040 --> 00:52:00,880
Do you think that could be incorpororporated into a call to action of like use code Black Friday?

543
00:52:01,200 --> 00:52:03,600
Yeah, like I'm probably going to do a Black Friday promotion.

544
00:52:03,680 --> 00:52:07,440
So it was top of mind, but use code whatever to get 20% off.

545
00:52:07,680 --> 00:52:10,400
Or do you think like on TikTok, that kind of thing just wouldn't work?

546
00:52:10,560 --> 00:52:19,200
Like use code whatever, even if it's incentivized like getting a discount, do you think that's too overt call to action for it to actually work on TikTok?

547
00:52:19,440 --> 00:52:21,680
I think if you get creative with it, it could definitely work.

548
00:52:22,160 --> 00:52:27,680
It just can't be the same way that you would sell the promo code through an influencer.

549
00:52:27,840 --> 00:52:31,680
Like I think it will always work with influencer marketing, like we were saying before.

550
00:52:32,000 --> 00:52:33,840
But for the scrappy creator marketing.

551
00:52:35,440 --> 00:52:42,000
on the for you page with zero trust with the audience says use promo code this at checkout for a discount, it feels like a reclame.

552
00:52:42,080 --> 00:52:43,040
It's not going to go viral.

553
00:52:43,760 --> 00:52:49,040
But imagine if you had the creator say, guys, did you see that code?

554
00:52:49,840 --> 00:52:51,120
Like, I just used it.

555
00:52:51,600 --> 00:52:56,000
Have you seen this hack that you can use to like get this thing for 50% off?

556
00:52:56,720 --> 00:53:02,320
Maybe that could, I don't know how legal that is, but I mean, there's probably other ways, you know.

557
00:53:02,960 --> 00:53:05,440
There's always creative ways that you can incorporate codes.

558
00:53:06,000 --> 00:53:07,680
So I could see some potential with that.

559
00:53:08,000 --> 00:53:09,840
Well, Joseph, it's been fascinating.

560
00:53:10,320 --> 00:53:11,040
I've learned a ton.

561
00:53:11,280 --> 00:53:12,640
I think this is going to be really interesting to folks.

562
00:53:13,360 --> 00:53:23,840
especially just this kind of new trend that we've talked about TikTok for years, but it does seem like people are figuring it out in a way that's different than it has been in the past.

563
00:53:24,320 --> 00:53:29,680
And I know you actually have a community of founders who are all working on this and trying to crack it.

564
00:53:29,840 --> 00:53:32,480
I actually joined a few weeks ago and that's part of how we met.

